// fs::make_absolute joins a relative path to the working directory as it is, `..` and all,
// and leaves an absolute one as it came; the empty path gives the working directory.
//
// The program is run in tmp/ with the paths `x/../y.ctx`, the empty one and tmp/sub's
// absolute one. Each is printed after `true`, that fs::is_absolute holds of the result.
// Then two lines the program checks itself: that the working directory is {dir}/tmp but for
// `\` against `/` and the case of letters, and that the first path is exactly the working
// directory, `/x/../y.ctx` after it.
//
// The working directory is printed as the system reports it: on Windows with `\` (and the
// drive's letter as the process was given it), so its head is `{any}` there and the program's
// own comparison states the rest; elsewhere it is {dir}/tmp exactly.
//# build abs: main.ctx
//# make dir tmp/sub
//# $ {abs} {dir}/tmp x/../y.ctx "" {dir}/tmp/sub
//# cwd: tmp
//# exit: 0
//# [windows] stdout:
//| true
//| {any}\work\Fs\make_absolute\tmp/x/../y.ctx
//| true
//| {any}\work\Fs\make_absolute\tmp
//| true
//| {dir}/tmp/sub
//| true
//| true
//# [posix] stdout:
//| true
//| {dir}/tmp/x/../y.ctx
//| true
//| {dir}/tmp
//| true
//| {dir}/tmp/sub
//| true
//| true
